out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Rasha has three things for the next twelve months and nearly wrote them as one list, and then noticed they are not the same kind of thing at all. The first she is going to do: take the B2 course in the spring — not an intention, because the form is in and the fee is paid, and if she does not go it will be because something has happened to her. The second she intends to do: train a second person on the pump settings. It is forty minutes, it is first on her own list, she has said it in a meeting — and nothing is arranged: no name, no date, no diary entry. Which, after 21.2, she knows exactly what it means: an intention with nothing attached to it is a feeling. The third she aims to do, and she says she is going to use the word aim because it is honest: she aims to be in a post where somebody else keeps the night record by the end of next year. She may not be; it depends on four people and one of them has not been appointed. Ms Huda said that is the whole distinction — going to is a decision taken and arranged, and you can name the arrangement; intend to is a settled purpose with nothing arranged yet, and it is the dangerous one because it feels like the first; and aim to is a target that depends on other people, said as a target, which is the honest word for about half of what anybody writes on a list like this. Nour asked what she does with the intention, and Rasha said what 21.2 says: attach something — forty minutes, one name, in the cover diary, before the end of February — and she will do it this week, because she has now said it in a room twice, and that is the point at which you either arrange it or admit it is a feeling. Then the last item, which is not a plan: the board came down in 23.7 and the first of June is still open, and today the class writes the last prediction of the course. Rasha\'s is the spring review of the blocks: before the end of May, and the minute again giving no reason, because the October minute gave none — one instance and a judgement, and she marked it as a judgement. What she will not know by then is whether anybody has read the error count.']));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('✗ I intend training a second person. → ✓ _intend **to train**_.   ✗ I aim at being in that post. → ✓ _aim **to be**_.   ✗ I am going to the B2 course take. → ✓ _I am going **to take** the B2 course_.   ✗ I am going to train somebody, but nothing is arranged. → ✓ then the verb is _**intend to**_ _(the test, not the error)_.   ✗ I will stop to say that the scheme covers four wards. → ✓ _stop **saying**_ _(20.4)_.   ✗ By the end of next year I aim to have been in that post. → ✓ _I aim **to be** in that post by the end of next year_.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** **The arrangement.** The form went in on 18 December and the first instalment is paid — _if I do not go, it will be because something has happened to me_. Note that the proof is an arrangement and not a degree of resolve.'));
out.push(A('**2** **Wafa\'a, the Thursday of the first week in February, the cover diary, forty minutes** — and **the diary entry goes in tomorrow morning**. The attachment is on the page and so is the date on which it will be made.'));
out.push(A('**3** Because the October minute gave no reason and that is **one** instance: a judgement with a single case behind it, and she marks it as one. The blind half is **whether anybody on the committee has read the error count** — the minute will not say, and nobody will tell her.'));
out.push(A('The paragraph to teach is the one about the verb: _I am writing_ aim _and not_ going to_, because a reader who cannot tell my arrangements from my hopes cannot use this page at all._ That sentence is what makes the honest verb read as strength rather than as hedging, and it is the sentence students need in order to be willing to write _aim_ at all. And note the line that marks two years of this course: **pattern or judgement**, said about her own reason, with a single case allowed to stand as a judgement.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
